{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-73842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1$ at the point $(1,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{1}{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1) \\\\ z-1 & =1\\cdot (x-1)+1\\cdot (y-1) \\\\ z-1 & =x+y-2 \\\\ -x-y+z+1 & =0 \\\\ x+y-z-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-73842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+y+z-3=0"] ["math" "x+y-z-1=0"] ["math" "x+y-z-2=0"] ["math" "x-y+z-1=0"] ["math" "x-y-z+1=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+y-z-1=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{y}^{3}$ at the point $(2,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}y)^{2}+1}\\cdot 2xy \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}y)^{2}+1}\\cdot {x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1}-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(2,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,0) & =\\frac{2(2)(0)}{(2)^{4}(0)^{2}+1}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,0) & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{(2)^{4}(0)^{2}+1}-3(0)^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,0)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,0) \\\\ z & =0(x-0)+4(y-0) \\\\ z & =4y \\\\ 4y-z & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-37715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-x+4y-z=0"] ["math" "2x+4y-z=0"] ["math" "4y+4x-z+1=0"] ["math" "4y-z=0"] ["math" "y+4x-z+1=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4y-z=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3$ at the point $(0,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(0,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,1) & =2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,1) & =-4(1)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,1) \\\\ z-1 & =0x-4(y-1) \\\\ z-1 & =-4y+4 \\\\ 4y+z-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-129439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x-z+1=0"] ["math" "3x+y-z=0"] ["math" "4x+y-1=0"] ["math" "4y+z-5=0"] ["math" "x+3y-z-2=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4y+z-5=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={y}^{2}{e}^{2x}-1$ at the point $(0,2,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}{e}^{2x}-1) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{2x}-1) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(0,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,2) & =2(2)^{2}{e}^{2(0)}=8 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,2) & =2(2){e}^{2(0)}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,2)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,2) \\\\ z-3 & =8(x-0)+4(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =8x+4y-8 \\\\ -8x-4y+z+5 & =0 \\\\ 8x+4y-z-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-37595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-8x+3y-z-3=0"] ["math" "-x+6y-z-3=0"] ["math" "3x+4y-z-5=0"] ["math" "4x+4y-z-5=0"] ["math" "8x+4y-z-5=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8x+4y-z-5=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=\\sqrt{xy}+xy$ at the point $(2,2,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{y}}+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(2,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}+2=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}+2=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-6 & =(x-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,2)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,2) \\\\ z-6 & =\\frac{5}{2}(x-2)+\\frac{5}{2}(y-2) \\\\ z-6 & =\\frac{5x}{2}+\\frac{5y}{2}-10 \\\\ 2z-12 & =5x+5y-20 \\\\ -5x-5y+2z+8 & =0 \\\\ 5x+5y-2z-8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-73841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5x+5y-2z+14=0"] ["math" "5x+5y-2z-8=0"] ["math" "5x+5y-z-4=0"] ["math" "5x-5y+2z-36=0"] ["math" "5x-5y-2z-8=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5x+5y-2z-8=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(yz)$ at the point $(-1,1,1,-\\pi ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(yz))=4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(yz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(yz))=\\frac{4xz}{1+{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(yz))=\\frac{4xy}{1+{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1,1) & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1\\cdot 1)=\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1,1) & =\\frac{4(-1)(1)}{1+({1}^{2})({1}^{2})}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,1,1) & =\\frac{4(-1)(1)}{1+({1}^{2})({1}^{2})}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-(-\\pi ) & =(x-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,1,1) \\\\ w+\\pi  & =\\pi (x+1)-2(y-1)-2(z-1) \\\\ w+\\pi  & =\\pi x-2y-2z+4+\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x-2y-2z-w+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-129485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x-y-w+\\pi +3=0"] ["math" "\\pi x-2y-2z-w+4=0"] ["math" "x+w+\\pi +1=0"] ["math" "x+y+\\pi z+w=0"] ["math" "x-y+4z+w+\\pi -2=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi x-2y-2z-w+4=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ at the point $(1,1,\\pi ,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi )+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi )+(z-\\pi )\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi (x-1)-\\pi (y-1)-(z-\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi x+\\pi -\\pi y+\\pi -z+\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-73851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi =0"] ["math" "\\pi x+\\pi y+z-w+3\\pi =0"] ["math" "\\pi x+\\pi y-z+w-\\pi =0"] ["math" "\\pi x-\\pi y+z+w-\\pi =0"] ["math" "\\pi x-\\pi y-z+w+\\pi =0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi =0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73852" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+\\sqrt{zx}$ at the point $(2,2,2,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73852" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+\\sqrt{zx})=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+\\sqrt{zx})=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{y}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+\\sqrt{zx})=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{z}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(2,2,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,2,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,2,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,2,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-6 & =(x-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,2,2)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,2,2)+(z-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,2,2) \\\\ w-6 & =(x-2)+(y-2)+(z-2) \\\\ w-6 & =x+y+z-6 \\\\ -x-y-z+w & =0 \\\\ x+y+z-w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73852" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-73852" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73852" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+y+z+w-12=0"] ["math" "x+y+z-w=0"] ["math" "x+y-z-w+4=0"] ["math" "x-y+z-w=0"] ["math" "x-y-z+w-4=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73852" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+y+z-w=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=z\\sqrt{y}-x$ at the point $(-1,4,1,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(-1,4,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,4,1) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,4,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,4,1) & =\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-3 & =(x-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,4,1)+(y-4)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,4,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,4,1) \\\\ w-3 & =-(x+1)+\\frac{1}{4}(y-4)+2(z-1) \\\\ w-3 & =-x+\\frac{1}{4}y+2z-4 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{4}y-2z+w+1 & =0 \\\\ 4x-y-8z+4w+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-129483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+3y-2z+w-11=0"] ["math" "2x+y-z-w+2=0"] ["math" "4x+2y+z+w-8=0"] ["math" "4x-y-8z+4w+4=0"] ["math" "4x-y-z+2w+3=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x-y-8z+4w+4=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z}$ at the point $(1,9,4,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,9,4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,9,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,9,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{9}}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,9,4) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-2 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,9,4)+(y-9)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,9,4)+(z-4)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,9,4) \\\\ w-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(y-9)-\\frac{1}{4}(z-4) \\\\ w-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-w+1 & =0 \\\\ 12(\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-w+1) & =12\\cdot 0 \\\\ 6x+2y-3z-12w+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-129476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x-z-8w+18=0"] ["math" "3x-y-2z-4w+22=0"] ["math" "6x+2y-3z-12w+12=0"] ["math" "6x-y-2z+6w-1=0"] ["math" "x-y+z-4w+12=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6x+2y-3z-12w+12=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{3}+xy$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+xy)=3{x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+xy)=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}+y,\\,x,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+y=0 \\\\ x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the second equation we get $x=0.$ Substituting $x=0$ into first equation, we obtain $y=0.$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={0}^{3}+0(0)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-129554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,1,-2)$ and $(1,1,2)$"] ["text" "$(-1,1,-2)$ only"] ["text" "$(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2)$"] ["text" "$(0,0,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(1,1,2)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-129554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,0,0)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the surface $z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩=⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-cy=8 \\\\ -xc=2\\pi  \\\\ -c=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-x(-2)=2\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $x=\\pi $ into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(-2)y=8\\,⟹\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\pi $ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -(\\pi )(2) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-37986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,6,8\\pi )"] ["math" "(2\\pi ,-\\pi ,2\\pi )"] ["math" "(2\\pi ,\\pi ,-1)"] ["math" "(2\\pi ,\\pi ,12\\pi )"] ["math" "(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the surface $z=4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6$  is the tangent plane \nhorizontal?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6) \\\\  & =8xy+{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨8xy+{y}^{3},\\,4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}8xy+{y}^{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0$ or ${y}^{2}+8x=0.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- If $y=0$, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+3x(0)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\n- If ${y}^{2}=-8x$, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+3x(-8x)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ back into ${y}^{2}=-8x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-8(0)\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSo, in both cases, we have $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Finally, substituting these values into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=4(0)^{2}(0)+(0)(0)^{3}-6=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,-6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-37990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,0,-6)"] ["math" "(0,0,-6)"] ["math" "(1,1,-1)"] ["text" "$(0,0,-6)$, $(1,0,-6)$"] ["text" "$(0,1,-6)$, $(1,0,-6)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-37990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,0,-6)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the surface $z=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨-2,4,2⟩?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,4,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{y}=-2 \\\\ cx{e}^{y}+2cy=4 \\\\ -c=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{e}^{y}=-2\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $y=0$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-2x{e}^{0}+2(-2)(0)=4\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2){e}^{0}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-2,0,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-73869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,0,-1)$ and $(1,0,1)$"] ["text" "$(-1,0,-1)$ only"] ["text" "$(-2,0,-2)$ and $(2,0,2)$"] ["text" "$(-2,0,-2)$ only"] ["text" "$(2,0,2)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-2,0,-2)$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2x-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2y+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x-2,\\,2y+6,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1$ while the second equation gives $y=-3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={1}^{2}-2(1)+(-3)^{2}+6(-3)+10=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(1,-3,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d8b0510cb7bfecfef5680fe1dc9555d5acb1db4207ecb5f6b3fa4c743518d13"} {"question" "q-73857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,3,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(-1,3,28)$ only"] ["text" "$(1,-3,0)$ and $(-1,3,28)$"] ["text" "$(1,-3,0)$ and $(3,1,20)$"] ["text" "$(1,-3,0)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "q-73857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(1,-3,0)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/state.json" "file_sha256" "6aec47a25cfe8fcfd81f7216275733b7e02c7b725012b1f7ec74459cbad58a50"} {"question" "e-6179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z= x^2 + xy$ at the point $(1,2, 3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/example-6179.json" "file_sha256" "b71dd53f7883897b86865756205ba946755accf2255d775761a82b50243a0685"} {"question" "e-6179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y) = (1,2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,2) & =2(1)+2=4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,2) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,2)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,2) \\\\ z-3 & =4(x-1)+1(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =4x+y-6 \\\\ -4x-y+z+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+y-z-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/example-6179.json" "file_sha256" "b71dd53f7883897b86865756205ba946755accf2255d775761a82b50243a0685"} {"question" "e-6185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=x^2+y^2+z^2-2xyz$ at the point $(1,-1,1,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/example-6185.json" "file_sha256" "34f099bd8451c3a5916ed484faaa5d0d1a0cc56be168c8402f0e0a8cfeaa76af"} {"question" "e-6185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0,w_0)$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw - w_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0,z_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0,z_0) + (z-z_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}(x_0,y_0,z_0).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2xyz)=2x-2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2xyz)=2y-2xz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2xyz)=2z-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z) = (1,-1,1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1) & =2(1)-2(-1)(1)=4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1) & =2(-1)-2(1)(1)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) & =2(1)-2(1)(-1)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-5 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1)+(y+1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-5 & =4(x-1)-4(y+1)+4(z-1) \\\\ w-5 & =4x-4y+4z-12 \\\\ -4x+4y-4z+w+7 & =0 \\\\ 4x-4y+4z-w-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/example-6185.json" "file_sha256" "34f099bd8451c3a5916ed484faaa5d0d1a0cc56be168c8402f0e0a8cfeaa76af"} {"question" "e-6182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the surface $z={y^2}+ {xy}$  is the tangent plane horizontal?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/example-6182.json" "file_sha256" "21d4f2174cb6f4aa52781a36d594d90e14ccfab61da881dad63a67a092956db5"} {"question" "e-6182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2y+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/assets/a20e553b10de1d00abd5beead7766a1859073f07b1f5bfb734b51e8433fbeee0.png) \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064554/example-6182.json" "file_sha256" "21d4f2174cb6f4aa52781a36d594d90e14ccfab61da881dad63a67a092956db5"}] "usage" {"e-6182" [] "e-6179" [] "q-37595" [] "e-6185" []} "basis" 2110 "retractions" [[17592186129549 :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z = y^{2}e^{2x} - 1$ at the point $(0, 2, 3)$."] [17592186128931 :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z= x^2 + xy$ at the point $(1,2, 3)$."] [17592186128931 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + xy) \\\\\n&= 2x + y \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + xy) \\\\\n&= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (1,2)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1, 2) &= 2(1) + 2 = 4 \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1, 2) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - 3 &= (x - 1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1, 2) + (y - 2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1, 2) \\\\\nz - 3 &= 4(x - 1) + 1(y - 2) \\\\\nz - 3 &= 4x + y - 6 \\\\\n- 4x - y + z + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n4x + y - z - 3 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128934 :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=x^2+y^2+z^2-2xyz$ at the point $(1,-1,1,5)$."] [17592186128934 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0,w_0)$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw - w_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0,z_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0,z_0) + (z-z_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}(x_0,y_0,z_0)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} - 2xyz) = 2x - 2yz \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} - 2xyz) = 2y - 2xz \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z} &= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} - 2xyz) = 2z - 2xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (1,-1,1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1, 1) &= 2(1) - 2(-1)(1) = 4 \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1, 1) &= 2(-1) - 2(1)(1) =-4 \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1, 1) &= 2(1) - 2(1)(-1) = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw - 5 &= (x - 1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1, 1) + (y + 1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1, 1) + (z - 1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1, 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw - 5 &= 4(x - 1) - 4(y + 1) + 4(z - 1) \\\\\nw - 5 &= 4x - 4y + 4z - 12 \\\\\n- 4x + 4y - 4z + w + 7 &= 0 \\\\\n4x - 4y + 4z - w - 7 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128932 :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z={y^2}+ {xy}$ is the tangent plane horizontal?"] [17592186128932 :question/worked-solution "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nrepresents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2} + xy) \\\\\n&= y \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2} + xy) \\\\\n&= 2y + x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0$:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂f}{∂x} = 0; \\frac{∂f}{∂y} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0$. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0)$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127089 17592186127090 17592186127091 17592186127092 17592186127093] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127088 :answer-fiel